        "original_target": "Free monoids on finite alphabets of different sizes are not isomorphic; discrete categories with different finite object counts are not equivalent. So a numeral/alphabet re-presentation cannot be an isomorphism of structure.",
        "source_scope_review": "The sources are general: Paper.lean and Paper001.lean prove, over List A and List alpha with hand-rolled structures, that an isomorphism induces an alphabet bijection (Classical.choice is used for it, per Paper.lean header) and hence equal sizes. Paper001.lean DiscreteEquiv is, by definition, a pair of mutually inverse maps on objects, so no_discrete_equivalence is the pigeonhole fact; the Python checks free_monoids_nonisomorphic and discrete_categories_inequivalent compare counts (the latter returns left != right). The DAG-local Lean theorem pg_to_sn_and_wdp_route_chain_composition is fixed arithmetic and vacuous for this node. This node re-proves the statement with Mathlib FreeMonoid / MulEquiv and Discrete categories (genuine categorical equivalence), bridges the source WordIso to MulEquiv, adds the converse iff, and records the sharp boundary: a set bijection exists, so the obstruction is multiplicativity; native: exact word counts, brute-force atoms, exhaustive homomorphism search with bounded image length, discrete equivalences, shortlex bijection.",
        "admitted_assumptions": []
      },
